<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#galilean-moons">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Galilean moons</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1610">1610</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Medicean Stars</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Galilean moons are the four largest moons of Jupiter, Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto, discovered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo Galilei</a> with his telescope in January 1610. He published the discovery in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidereus-nuncius">Sidereus Nuncius</a> and named them the Medicean Stars in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-ii-de-medici">Cosimo II de' Medici</a> and his brothers, winning a post at the Tuscan court. Bodies orbiting another planet supported the Copernican view that not everything circles the Earth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galilean-moons.jpg" alt="Galilean moons" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: WhatADrag07 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jupiter_and_the_Galilean_Satellites_(True_Color).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
